import Verified.Examples.Fma

/-! The forty-sixth program of the verified compiler: `tgamma`, CORE-MATH's correctly rounded `Γ`,
ported from `src/binary64/tgamma/tgamma.c` at commit `ff2f71f635e95d395a6646b7e7b2c72e7b03a5d8` of
https://gitlab.inria.fr/core-math/core-math (Copyright (c) 2024 Alexei Sibidanov, MIT license).  The
code evaluates `Γ` in double-double arithmetic with an error bound `eps` and returns the result when
`h + (l + eps)` and `h + (l - eps)` round to the same double.  Below `1/4` in magnitude the fast
path is `1/x` plus a polynomial, from -3 to 4 a polynomial about the nearest `k + 1/2` times or
divided by a product of shifts, above 4 `exp (lgamma x)` from Stirling's series, and below -3 the
reflection `Γ x = π/(sin (π x) Γ (1 - x))`.  When the test fails, `as_tgamma_accurate` evaluates to
about 100 bits with polynomials on nine ranges, and a table of 57 inputs that even that path cannot
round supplies their results.  Every `__builtin_fma` calls `Fma.fma`, the correctly rounded fused
multiply-add, so the port computes CORE-MATH's results bit for bit.

The port follows the C code operation for operation, with these changes.  A C function that returns
a second result through a pointer returns a pair.  The tables are words in eight arrays, which
`tgamma` passes to `tgammaWith`, and their docstrings give the offsets of the C arrays.  The C
integers `i`, `jm`, `ip`, and `k` that count or select are integer-valued doubles, and the exponents
`e` and `eoff` and the index `k` of `as_expd` are two's complement words, with `sar` for C's
arithmetic shift.  `roundeven_finite` adds and subtracts `2 ^ 52`, which rounds ties to even, and
`floor` follows from it.  The table of hard cases is scanned in full instead of by binary search,
with the same result.  The dialect has no floating-point flags and no `errno`, so `raise_underflow`
and the assignments to `errno` are gone, and the expressions that C evaluates for their flags keep
their values.  Each constant is the bit pattern of its value, with the C literal in a comment.  The
`verified_compile` call at the end of the module is commented out: it waits for a change of the
reflector, whose time and memory for a function grow faster than linearly with its number of `let`
bindings, so that this module's call exceeds the build's memory limit.  The journal records the
measurements.  `TgammaAccuracy.lean` checks the results against a reference computed with Lean's
integers. -/

